PKG -> code + dependency 
ex: python, SQL, Mongo, Nginx 
- The Package manager does the installation, Updatation, and Deletion 
- For Debian, we have two package manager 
  1. APT (Advanced Package Tool)- > Install both the code and Dependency
  2. DPKG (Debian Pkg Manager) -> Install only the code 
- we use the low-level DPKG when we are working in scale applications it better to use the low-level packages 
- apt upgrade 
- apt install 
- apt install  -y <name> then we do not have to deal with answering the yes-no questions 

-For Red Hat - YUM (YellowDog update modified) - years ago, it was only called Yellowdog update

Update - Servers info syncs with the central repo 

Upgrade - also installing 

nginx - is a proxy


User Management:
 - Users
    - useradd -m <name>
    - usermod -a(append add the uses to groups without removing from the present group) -G(add the list of supplemanry groups) <group> <user>


 - Groups 
    - groupadd <name>

- Check here to locate we can see cat /etc/passwd
                                    cat /etc/group

- id (**root has 0**)

Permissions:
-> -rw- r-- r--
- the first - is for the type (regular file or a directory)
- r - read - 4
- w - write - 2
- x - execute - 1
-> -rw is the permission for the ROOT user 
-> r-- is the permission for the group
-> r-- is the permission for others 
- Precedence of rules -> The users take more precedence over the group 
- CHMOD 744 -> 
- when we are changing permission, we write chowm <user>:<newowner> file name
chmod 7(4+2+1)4(4)4(4) 
- chowm is used to change the ownership of the file 
- The syntax for chown is:
  chown user:group filename
  There are 3 main ways to use it (from the class examples):
  
  Change only user ownership:
  chown username filename
  Change only group ownership:
  chown :groupname filename
  Change both user and group ownership:
  chown username:groupname filename


